  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA warm front will gradually lift northward through the forecast\narea early this morning. The modest forcing for ascent coupled\nwith PWATS climbing to around 1.75 inches will aid in showers,\npossibly heavy at times. A thunderstorm is possible. Latest CAMs\nhave been a bit inconsistent with the potential for a 1 to 3\ninch band of rain early this morning across the mountain zones.\nHeavier amounts would be possible. However, the inconsistencies\nin the latest guidance is leading to less confidence in the\noccurrence of this. We'll monitor this for a localized flash\nflood but appears as though the most focused forcing will be to\nthe north of the CWA.\n\nAs today progresses, low pressure will develop to our south and\npass northeastward into the Gulf of Maine tonight. This will aid\nin turning our winds toward a more northerly component which\nwill also aid in lowering PWATS as the mid levels dry out. This\nwill result in a more spotty nature to the showers and possibly\nportend to drizzle at times along with some fog on the coastal\nplain. \n\nIt's not out of the question that the CT River valley \ndestabilizes by late this afternoon which could aid in\nthunderstorm development for a couple of hours before sunset.\n\nTonight will feature areas of fog and drizzle with low clouds.\n\nGradual improvement is foreseen Tuesday, especially during the \nafternoon hours.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nTuesday, low level moisture may still be entrenched along the \ncoast and interior. It will depend on proximity to a low as it \nexits southern New England and pivots towards/just outside of \nthe Gulf of Maine. Would expect northerly flow behind the low to\nwhittle this away, but just 10 kts at 850mb won't make it \nquick.\n\nGuidance has been initiating some diurnal showers or storms \nTuesday afternoon across portions of the interior where this \nmoisture overlaps enough daytime heating. Model profiles show \nmoisture in the lower levels, but also feature a very dry mid to\nupper level. It is possible warm air aloft also advects in, \ncreating a cap and ultimately limiting a unstable layer. As a \nresult, went with lower precip chances at this range. Not ruling\nout showers and storms, but think they could be more isolated \nin nature given the prominent dry air aloft and ceiling on \ninstability.\n\nWarmer for Wednesday as southerly flow kicks in ahead of an \nelongated cold front. Highs should bump up into the low 80s, a \nfew degrees warmer than Tuesday. While the approaching front \nwill carry precipitation with it, lift is in question as the \nparent surface low is well north, east of Hudson Bay. Thus \nprecip into New England may be dependent on daytime instability \nif timing aligns. \n\nA southern stream disturbance may pass just to the south\nThursday, exiting across the Gulf of Maine or open Atlantic.\nThis could bring NW periphery showers, but we could also see a\ndeep enough dry layer to negate much of this. High pressure then\nlooks to return to the region late week, perhaps in time for a\nsunny start to next weekend.",
